00:23:54.430 --> 00:23:57.060
in your back screen, that's marketing.

00:23:57.060 --> 00:23:58.420
You hacked my attention.

00:23:58.420 --> 00:23:59.750
I'm impressed, brother.

00:23:59.750 --> 00:24:00.970
Let me know if you need a job.

00:24:00.970 --> 00:24:02.020
All right keep going.

00:24:03.050 --> 00:24:05.680
A question from a couple
of college students,

00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:09.764
obviously it's a weird time
to enter the job market.

00:24:09.764 --> 00:24:12.620
It's a weird time to be feeling ambitious

00:24:12.620 --> 00:24:16.110
and have a lot of doubt
and uncertainty around

00:24:16.110 --> 00:24:17.500
the start to your career.

00:24:17.500 --> 00:24:19.960
What do you have to say as far as advice

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:23.033
for some of the people entering
the job market right now?

00:24:24.520 --> 00:24:27.470
- You don't get to choose what
era you come out of college.

00:24:29.508 --> 00:24:32.180
You know, there's people on
here that are like these kids,

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:34.530
I came out in 2008 and nine
during the great recession,

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:35.930
there were no jobs.

00:24:35.930 --> 00:24:38.060
That's number one, number two,

00:24:38.060 --> 00:24:42.720
you're either winning or losing
in your perspective on life.

00:24:42.720 --> 00:24:44.200
The amount of people that are looking

00:24:44.200 --> 00:24:46.630
to hire people to do TikTok.

00:24:46.630 --> 00:24:50.243
TikTok Creative in 2021 is remarkable.

00:24:51.290 --> 00:24:53.970
And who do you think they
think knows how to do that?

00:24:53.970 --> 00:24:58.350
All the young people in the
world, even though they may not.

00:24:58.350 --> 00:24:59.740
So I would argue that this isn't one

00:24:59.740 --> 00:25:01.530
of the greatest eras ever

00:25:01.530 --> 00:25:04.080
to go from college into the workforce,

00:25:04.080 --> 00:25:06.040
because we have an explosion

00:25:06.040 --> 00:25:10.250
of companies hiring content
creators for digital and social,

00:25:10.250 --> 00:25:13.790
and they assume the best ones
are the kids out of school.

00:25:13.790 --> 00:25:16.540
I think that people are
going to find a humongous,

00:25:16.540 --> 00:25:18.690
humongous market.

00:25:18.690 --> 00:25:21.130
If they are thoughtful about
what they want to get into,

00:25:21.130 --> 00:25:23.300
and are not rigid and saying,

00:25:23.300 --> 00:25:24.630
well I have to get a job in this.

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:26.420
If you have some humility and patience,

00:25:26.420 --> 00:25:28.780
and you take the jobs that
are there in front of you,

00:25:28.780 --> 00:25:30.573
I think there's a huge opportunity.

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:37.440
- Another question on the
sort of job market careers

00:25:37.440 --> 00:25:41.140
sort of angle is, a lot
of people are asking

00:25:41.140 --> 00:25:45.160
what's been your most effective
way to find motivation

00:25:45.160 --> 00:25:49.330
in time of either feeling stagnant

00:25:49.330 --> 00:25:51.543
or maybe feeling uninspired or stuck?

00:25:55.550 --> 00:25:57.120
- Practicality,

00:25:57.120 --> 00:25:59.490
being practical about the situation,

00:25:59.490 --> 00:26:03.033
and suffocating entitlement.

00:26:04.710 --> 00:26:08.380
I think everybody here needs
to realize that for all of us,

00:26:08.380 --> 00:26:12.000
we had the great fortune of being alive

00:26:13.030 --> 00:26:18.030
and living in America during
the prime of its empireship.

00:26:18.850 --> 00:26:21.330
And if you look at the history of man

00:26:21.330 --> 00:26:24.400
over the last hundreds and
hundreds and hundreds of years,

00:26:24.400 --> 00:26:29.053
the reason those kinds of
situations tend to lose their way

00:26:29.053 --> 00:26:31.223
because people get entitled.

00:26:32.370 --> 00:26:34.890
People are like crying about things,

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:39.280
that people would literally love to have

00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:40.280
just a hundred years ago.

00:26:40.280 --> 00:26:43.080
I mean, there's been
harder times than COVID.

00:26:43.080 --> 00:26:47.063
We are soft, because we're an empire.

00:26:47.990 --> 00:26:51.530
And I think when you understand
that and take a step back,

00:26:51.530 --> 00:26:55.230
you can get a lot more grateful,

00:26:55.230 --> 00:26:57.900
and realize this will evolve.

00:26:57.900 --> 00:27:01.180
And the perspective one deploys

00:27:01.180 --> 00:27:03.680
against time like this really matters.

00:27:03.680 --> 00:27:06.750
Instead of being upset
for what you don't have,

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:08.690
let's be happy for what you have.

00:27:08.690 --> 00:27:11.310
A lot of people died during this pandemic.

00:27:11.310 --> 00:27:13.170
If you or someone you love didn't,

00:27:13.170 --> 00:27:14.940
that's already a huge win.

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:18.960
Next, a lot of you have
rekindled your relationships

00:27:18.960 --> 00:27:20.943
with your children or your plus ones.

00:27:21.810 --> 00:27:24.010
Others have learned that their plus ones

00:27:24.010 --> 00:27:26.070
might not be for them in the longterm.

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:28.310
And the fact that you weren't
around each other all the time

00:27:28.310 --> 00:27:29.820
is why you were sustainable.

00:27:29.820 --> 00:27:33.163
And as much as that may
hurt, and may sounds silly.

00:27:35.530 --> 00:27:37.430
It's a real gift to realize that,

00:27:37.430 --> 00:27:38.570
and hopefully both of those individuals

00:27:38.570 --> 00:27:39.710
can go and become happy.

00:27:39.710 --> 00:27:41.370
There's a lot of great things
that have happened here.

00:27:41.370 --> 00:27:42.910
I also want to remind everybody,

00:27:42.910 --> 00:27:45.230
that had COVID started two months earlier,

00:27:45.230 --> 00:27:48.270
Kobe Bryant would probably
be alive for 50 more years.

00:27:48.270 --> 00:27:51.060
There was a lot of people destined to die

00:27:51.060 --> 00:27:53.560
in a traffic accident, over the last year

00:27:53.560 --> 00:27:56.640
that lives were saved because
they were stuck in their home.

00:27:56.640 --> 00:27:59.480
To me, this is glass half
full versus glass half empty.

00:27:59.480 --> 00:28:02.293
And that's how I approach
these kinds of conversations.

00:28:05.090 --> 00:28:07.457
- It's a really, really
good way to look at it.

00:28:07.457 --> 00:28:10.754
And it's interesting to
think about it like that.

00:28:10.754 --> 00:28:13.990
There's also a changing gears slightly,

00:28:13.990 --> 00:28:16.760
but I wanted to talk a little bit about,

00:28:16.760 --> 00:28:18.180
we hit on it slightly earlier,

00:28:18.180 --> 00:28:21.603
but your idea of Santa Clausing
in a couple of meetings,

00:28:22.510 --> 00:28:24.660
I love it, I think it's an awesome idea.

00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:26.810
We got a lot of questions about,

00:28:26.810 --> 00:28:30.503
what separates good leaders
who get the job done,

00:28:30.503 --> 00:28:31.713
from a great one?

00:28:35.250 --> 00:28:36.203
- Intent.

00:28:39.470 --> 00:28:41.870
You know, like what are you about?

00:28:41.870 --> 00:28:44.060
You know, I'm trying to get every person

00:28:44.060 --> 00:28:46.070
that I have ever spent
any kind of time with

00:28:46.070 --> 00:28:47.140
to come to my funeral.

00:28:47.140 --> 00:28:49.240
That is my number one KPI.

00:28:49.240 --> 00:28:50.930
That was my number one KPI in my life.

00:28:50.930 --> 00:28:53.910
My number two KPI, is enjoying the process

00:28:53.910 --> 00:28:56.410
of trying to buy the New
York Jets professionally.

00:28:57.960 --> 00:29:00.520
That's a framework that allows
me to be a great leader.

00:29:00.520 --> 00:29:02.880
You know, like it,

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:07.880
I don't care about
anything other than legacy,

00:29:11.070 --> 00:29:13.630
which allows me to make human decisions

00:29:13.630 --> 00:29:15.120
instead of financial decisions,

00:29:15.120 --> 00:29:16.700
every time I'm passed with them.

00:29:16.700 --> 00:29:20.980
I also had the great
luxury of being raised

00:29:20.980 --> 00:29:24.350
by a parent, my mom, and
my parents were together.

00:29:24.350 --> 00:29:25.969
My dad just worked all the time.

00:29:25.969 --> 00:29:28.670
Who instilled disproportionate
self-esteem in me,

00:29:28.670 --> 00:29:31.950
but also suffocated entitlement.

00:29:31.950 --> 00:29:33.840
So I have confidence

00:29:33.840 --> 00:29:35.540
but I don't think I deserve anything.

00:29:35.540 --> 00:29:37.540
And if I disappear tomorrow,

00:29:37.540 --> 00:29:39.910
I don't think I meant anything
regardless of the accolades.

00:29:39.910 --> 00:29:40.743
I mean it.

00:29:40.743 --> 00:29:44.220
I'll have hours of good
RIP Gary V comments,

00:29:44.220 --> 00:29:48.097
and social and a story
here or two and what?

00:29:48.097 --> 00:29:51.970
Like, you know, like I think
when you have humility,

00:29:51.970 --> 00:29:54.440
but you layer it with
ambition and kindness

00:29:54.440 --> 00:29:56.570
you can create a real great formula.

00:29:56.570 --> 00:30:01.570
And I think how real what
just came out of my mouth is,

00:30:02.210 --> 00:30:04.433
is the Delta between good and great.

00:30:06.130 --> 00:30:08.018
And I think I can end up being great

00:30:08.018 --> 00:30:08.940
because I really am not joking to you.

00:30:08.940 --> 00:30:11.880
And I think a lot of people
say what I just said,

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:13.330
and don't actually act on it.

00:30:15.830 --> 00:30:18.310
I don't think people are
trickable on the record.

00:30:18.310 --> 00:30:20.180
I know people might find that stunning.

00:30:20.180 --> 00:30:22.393
I don't think it's a sustainable game.

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:25.593
And so I live by that.

00:30:27.500 --> 00:30:30.280
- When you talk about sort of humility,

00:30:30.280 --> 00:30:33.590
and being human in
career in the workforce,

00:30:33.590 --> 00:30:36.270
there's a lot of people that get,

00:30:36.270 --> 00:30:40.190
either disillusioned or
worried or apprehensive about

00:30:40.190 --> 00:30:43.610
needing to hit numbers and
coming off as disingenuine

00:30:43.610 --> 00:30:48.210
or needing to create something out of

00:30:48.210 --> 00:30:50.920
what a persona that might
not be themselves, right?

00:30:50.920 --> 00:30:55.740
So a lot of the questions that
we saw were, as sales reps,

00:30:55.740 --> 00:30:58.370
let's look at that
specifically as the lens,

00:30:58.370 --> 00:31:01.710
how do you continue to
be authentic and genuine

00:31:01.710 --> 00:31:04.973
but also needed to drive
revenue for your company?

00:31:05.990 --> 00:31:08.873
- By quitting if you don't
believe in what you're selling.

00:31:13.180 --> 00:31:15.870
You can't imagine how much I love selling,

00:31:15.870 --> 00:31:17.560
because I sell shit I believe in.

00:31:17.560 --> 00:31:21.020
Whether that was some weird
Pinot Noir from Oregon

00:31:21.020 --> 00:31:22.653
when I was in the wine business,

00:31:23.850 --> 00:31:27.680
whether that was the
KSWISS sneakers I created,

00:31:27.680 --> 00:31:29.480
whether that's wine text,

00:31:29.480 --> 00:31:31.363
whether that's VaynerMedia.

00:31:33.410 --> 00:31:37.260
I think it's really hard
for people in this room,

00:31:37.260 --> 00:31:38.520
if you don't believe in your product

00:31:38.520 --> 00:31:40.470
and the thing that you're offering,

00:31:40.470 --> 00:31:42.420
you're in a shitty spot, right?

00:31:42.420 --> 00:31:45.020
Cause now, because you
inherently are not authentic,

00:31:45.020 --> 00:31:46.900
the game is over before you started.

00:31:46.900 --> 00:31:48.090
If you think you're selling somebody

00:31:48.090 --> 00:31:52.015
an obstructed view seat

00:31:52.015 --> 00:31:54.170
because you got to move the inventory,

00:31:54.170 --> 00:31:56.820
or if you are trying to sell a suite

00:31:56.820 --> 00:31:59.550
and you know they're grossly overpriced

00:31:59.550 --> 00:32:01.920
because it's year 13 in your venue,

00:32:01.920 --> 00:32:06.250
and the fixtures aren't as
nice as they used to be,

00:32:06.250 --> 00:32:08.253
the liquor's a little diluted down,

00:32:09.370 --> 00:32:11.230
no more Grey goose, that's now Smirnoff,

00:32:11.230 --> 00:32:12.850
You know what I mean?

00:32:12.850 --> 00:32:14.570
You got to believe in
what you fucking sell.

00:32:14.570 --> 00:32:17.090
And, and by the way,

00:32:17.090 --> 00:32:19.300
I'm not interested in
sitting in my high horse.

00:32:19.300 --> 00:32:20.540
If you actually sitting here and saying,

00:32:20.540 --> 00:32:22.970
I actually don't, but fuck Gary.

00:32:22.970 --> 00:32:24.560
like, I can't quit my job.

00:32:24.560 --> 00:32:25.800
You don't have to do it right now,

00:32:25.800 --> 00:32:28.293
but you need to recognize it's true.

00:32:28.293 --> 00:32:31.180
And you should start the
process of figuring that out.

00:32:31.180 --> 00:32:34.730
Otherwise you're going to
be a cliche salesperson,

00:32:34.730 --> 00:32:36.180
and they're going to feel it.

00:32:38.020 --> 00:32:40.930
- Yeah I think we're going to
bring up the next person now.

00:32:40.930 --> 00:32:44.050
And Chanelle from The
Washington Football Team.

00:32:44.050 --> 00:32:46.650
I wanted to ask you to
unmute and ask your question.

00:32:48.010 --> 00:32:50.642
- All right, yeah so Gary,
you have a very strong message

00:32:50.642 --> 00:32:52.770
as it relates to authenticity.

00:32:52.770 --> 00:32:54.860
So, you know, what
advice would you give us

00:32:54.860 --> 00:32:58.470
on how to build authentic
client relationships

00:32:58.470 --> 00:33:00.203
without coming off as phony?

00:33:01.638 --> 00:33:04.570
- You know, I like to
tell people the truth.

00:33:04.570 --> 00:33:07.330
Rachel if you came to me and
I was DC Wine Business, right?

00:33:07.330 --> 00:33:09.280
And you're like, look, I'm
trying to get your business.

00:33:09.280 --> 00:33:11.870
I'm like, I respect that.

00:33:11.870 --> 00:33:13.460
I'm like, let's talk about it.

00:33:13.460 --> 00:33:15.620
You know, like, hey, I'm about The Skins

00:33:15.620 --> 00:33:16.700
and I want to be on the field,

00:33:16.700 --> 00:33:18.130
and you're going to trick me with that.

00:33:18.130 --> 00:33:19.993
Then that's going to be the real hook

00:33:19.993 --> 00:33:21.430
why buy that $80,000 sponsorship.

00:33:21.430 --> 00:33:23.050
But like, can you make it worth

00:33:23.050 --> 00:33:24.750
for my business a little bit better?

00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:28.060
You know, like just real, it works.

00:33:28.060 --> 00:33:30.400
And by the way, you're
talking to somebody at 13

00:33:30.400 --> 00:33:33.642
who was more full of shit
than anybody I've ever met.

00:33:33.642 --> 00:33:35.390
I had a lot of gift of gab.

00:33:35.390 --> 00:33:37.620
I was a raw street kid
that came from nothing

00:33:37.620 --> 00:33:39.233
and I was just trying to win.

00:33:40.242 --> 00:33:42.623
So I know what it looks like.

00:33:43.940 --> 00:33:46.880
And honesty and candor, and ambition,

00:33:46.880 --> 00:33:49.650
and kindness and listening,
listening is a beast.

00:33:49.650 --> 00:33:53.920
You know, I'm so confusing to
people because I talk so much,

00:33:53.920 --> 00:33:55.140
I have a certain way of talking.

00:33:55.140 --> 00:33:56.730
I'm sure there's people here
that have cursed a little bit

00:33:56.730 --> 00:33:58.740
that don't like, I respect that.

00:33:58.740 --> 00:34:01.460
I come from where I come
from, I am who I am.

00:34:01.460 --> 00:34:04.273
But every minute I'm not
doing this, I'm listening.

00:34:06.110 --> 00:34:08.920
And in that listening, it's
really made me powerful.

00:34:08.920 --> 00:34:10.860
And I think listening, right?

00:34:10.860 --> 00:34:14.920
If you see a big business in
your region going on TikTok

00:34:14.920 --> 00:34:17.013
and going am, you're listening.

00:34:17.880 --> 00:34:18.713
Got it?

00:34:18.713 --> 00:34:20.460
So when you call and be like Carl,

00:34:20.460 --> 00:34:21.470
you're killing it on TikTok.

00:34:21.470 --> 00:34:23.090
Do you want to be our first sponsor

00:34:23.090 --> 00:34:25.670
on our pregame TikTok campaign?

00:34:25.670 --> 00:34:26.503
You see where I'm going?

00:34:26.503 --> 00:34:28.410
You're innovating, you're
watching, you're listening,

00:34:28.410 --> 00:34:30.550
you're watching, you're listening.

00:34:30.550 --> 00:34:32.880
Don't come with the same
playbook to everybody.

00:34:32.880 --> 00:34:34.423
Contextualize that shit.

00:34:40.230 --> 00:34:41.640
- Next, we are going to-

00:34:41.640 --> 00:34:43.490
- By the way Carl said radical candor.

00:34:43.490 --> 00:34:45.840
And Carl, I look at it as kind candor.

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:47.800
I've got a little bit of a
slightly different tweak,

00:34:47.800 --> 00:34:49.470
because candor can be tough.

00:34:49.470 --> 00:34:52.030
Because people are insecure
and they get nervous.

00:34:52.030 --> 00:34:54.710
I've been thrown around
something called kind candor,

00:34:54.710 --> 00:34:56.070
which is you're going
to shoot it straight,

00:34:56.070 --> 00:35:00.330
come with compassion and
empathy, and nice and tender.

00:35:00.330 --> 00:35:02.480
So really important
part of being a leader.

00:35:02.480 --> 00:35:04.890
If you got to tell, you know,

00:35:04.890 --> 00:35:06.430
if Alicia Lynch has to tell somebody

00:35:06.430 --> 00:35:07.680
that they're not performing,

00:35:07.680 --> 00:35:10.341
if you can come with
kindness around that candor,

00:35:10.341 --> 00:35:11.174
you have a much better shot

00:35:11.174 --> 00:35:12.613
because when you just go with candor,

00:35:12.613 --> 00:35:16.090
most people will take it
and drive with insecurity

00:35:16.090 --> 00:35:17.290
and get fear-based.

00:35:17.290 --> 00:35:19.040
And it throws off the whole system.

00:35:20.550 --> 00:35:23.680
You can't do matter of fact
candor, because 90% of people

00:35:23.680 --> 00:35:25.590
are gonna start looking
for a job an hour later.

00:35:25.590 --> 00:35:27.290
Cause they think you're
gonna fire them in a month.

00:35:27.290 --> 00:35:29.690
Even though you thought
about it as a good thing,

00:35:29.690 --> 00:35:32.490
you got to come with kind candor.

00:35:32.490 --> 00:35:34.770
Hey, Carl, you just been here six months.

00:35:34.770 --> 00:35:35.603
You know what I mean?

00:35:35.603 --> 00:35:36.436
I get it.

00:35:36.436 --> 00:35:38.020
You know, maybe you're
struggling with COVID.

00:35:38.020 --> 00:35:38.853
I'm here for you.

00:35:38.853 --> 00:35:39.686
How do I make it better?

00:35:39.686 --> 00:35:41.410
Because I'm seeing this shortcoming.

00:35:44.802 --> 00:35:46.900
By the way, on the record,
the greatest vulnerability

00:35:46.900 --> 00:35:49.400
of my entire 25 year career, candor.

00:35:49.400 --> 00:35:52.630
I'm incredibly candorous
on stage, like right now,

00:35:52.630 --> 00:35:55.250
and for my community, but as a manager,

00:35:55.250 --> 00:35:59.690
because my father, was
a mean candor deliverer,

00:35:59.690 --> 00:36:01.650
I demonized candor as a child.

00:36:01.650 --> 00:36:04.420
I didn't realize it, probably
the last two three years,

00:36:04.420 --> 00:36:05.850
recognition for me.

00:36:05.850 --> 00:36:07.040
And so I'm on my journey,

00:36:07.040 --> 00:36:10.370
and I see the I see the benefits,

00:36:10.370 --> 00:36:11.910
and it was a real vulnerability.

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:14.660
Candor is a vulnerability,
because then you're bullshit.

00:36:14.660 --> 00:36:16.020
And bullshit's bad.

00:36:16.020 --> 00:36:19.700
But I just also recognize most
people struggled with candor.

00:36:19.700 --> 00:36:22.083
So this kind candor really matters.

